.TH KARIDNS\-TAPSTAT 1 "2026-11-13" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/karidns-tapstat.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
karidns\-tapstat \- Aggregates the dnstap stream of \fBkaridns\fR into periodic
query statistics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-tapstat \-s socket [options]
karidns\-tapstat \-r file [options]
karidns\-tapstat \-V | \-h
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBkaridns\-tapstat\fR is the "query analytics" tool of KariDNS. The server does
not analyse queries itself (nothing is added to the query path beyond the
existing counters and dnstap <https://dnstap.info/>); this separate program
consumes the dnstap stream and counts, per interval:
.IP \(bu 2
the \fBzone\fR the query belongs to,
.IP \(bu 2
the query name (\fBqname\fR),
.IP \(bu 2
the query type (\fBqtype\fR),
.IP \(bu 2
the response code (\fBrcode\fR), and
.IP \(bu 2
the \fBclient prefix\fR (the client address cut to /24 for IPv4 and /56 for
IPv6).
.PP
It works in one of two ways:
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-s socket\fR \[u2014] it listens on a UNIX domain socket as a \fBFrame
Streams\fR receiver. \fBkaridns\fR (or any dnstap sender) connects to that
socket (\fBdnstap { socket "..."; }\fR), the two sides run the bidirectional
handshake (the sender's READY, the receiver's ACCEPT, the sender's START), data
frames follow, and the sender ends with STOP, which the receiver answers with
FINISH. Every \fB\-i\fR seconds it writes a summary and starts over. A sender
that goes away is simply reconnected by \fBkaridns\fR; several senders may be
connected at once (up to 16), and their frames are added together.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\-r file\fR \[u2014] it reads a Frame Streams file (START, data frames,
optional STOP; \fB\-\fR reads standard input) and writes one summary at the end.
.PP
The summary goes to standard output, or, with \fB\-o\fR, into a file that is
replaced atomically. Nothing is uploaded or sent anywhere: files and standard
output only.
.PP
\fBkaridns\-tapstat\fR is an ordinary unprivileged process (it is not in
Capsicum capability mode). Run it as its own user. It is a POSIX program
(FreeBSD, Linux, macOS); it is not part of the Windows build of \fBdag\fR.
.SS Input is untrusted
Everything on the socket and in the file is treated as hostile: Frame Streams
lengths are capped before a buffer is sized from them (data frames 256 KiB,
control frames 512 bytes), every protobuf field is bounds-checked, only the DNS
header and question are read from the embedded DNS message, and a sender that
breaks the protocol (data before START, a wrong content type, an oversized
frame, ...) has its connection closed with a message on standard error. A sender
that does not finish the handshake within 5 seconds is dropped. The content type
accepted is \fBprotobuf:dnstap.Dnstap\fR.
.SS What is counted
\fBkaridns\fR sends \fBAUTH_QUERY\fR and \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR messages (\fBdnstap
{ log\-queries yes; log\-responses yes; }\fR; both by default).
.IP \(bu 2
The zone, qname, qtype and client tables count the messages of one type, chosen
with \fB\-t\fR: \fBresponse\fR (default) or \fBquery\fR. With \fB\-t response\fR
the messages of the other type are only counted in the totals. If \fBkaridns\fR
is set to log queries only, use \fB\-t query\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
The rcode table always counts \fBAUTH_RESPONSE\fR messages (a query has no
response code). It is the low four bits of the DNS header (RFC 1035 section
4.1.1); the EDNS extended part (RFC 6891) is not added.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBZone\fR: with \fB\-z\fR (or \fB\-Z\fR) the zone is the longest listed name
that the query name is at or below (compared label by label, case-insensitive);
a name below none of them is \fBother\fR. Without a zone list, the zone that the
server put into the dnstap message (\fBquery_zone\fR) is used; a message without
one (REFUSED, ...) is \fBother\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBClient prefix\fR: the \fBquery_address\fR of the message. An IPv4-mapped IPv6
address (\fB::ffff:a.b.c.d\fR) is counted as IPv4. \fB\-4\fR and \fB\-6\fR
change the prefix lengths.
.IP \(bu 2
The query name is lower-cased and written in master-file notation (a dot inside
a label as \fB\e.\fR, unprintable bytes as \fB\eDDD\fR). It is read from the
question of the message, which must not be compressed; a message whose question
is malformed is counted under \fBmalformed\fR and not in the name tables.
.SS Bounded memory
Each of the five tables holds at most \fB\-k\fR keys (default 10000; qname keys
take about 280 bytes each, so \fB\-k 1000000\fR needs roughly 300 MB for that
table; the other tables are much smaller). The tables use the \fBSpace-Saving\fR
algorithm (Metwally, Agrawal and El Abbadi, "Efficient Computation of Frequent
and Top-k Elements in Data Streams", 2005): while there is room a new key is
added; when the table is full, a key that is not in it replaces the kept key
with the smallest count and inherits that count, then counts its own
event. Memory is fixed by \fB\-k\fR (a min-heap and a hash index, nothing
allocated per event, O(log k) per event). The result is a list of the heaviest
keys of the interval that does not depend on the order of arrival: a heavy name
that first appears after the table filled up still gets into the list. The price
is that a count may be too high. For every listed key the true count lies
between \fBcount \- error\fR and \fBcount\fR; \fBerror\fR is the count the key
inherited when it replaced another key (0 for a key that was never in
doubt). Every key whose true count is above \fBevents / k\fR is certainly in the
list, and the counts of all kept keys add up to \fBevents\fR. \fBevictions\fR is
the number of events that replaced a key; with 0 evictions all counts are
exact. Choose \fB\-k\fR large enough that the keys you care about stay well
above \fBevents / k\fR. The tables are reset after every summary, so memory
never grows with time. The hash of the tables is keyed with a random value drawn
at start, so names cannot be chosen to collide.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP 8
\fB\-s socket\fR
Listen on this UNIX socket. A stale socket file of an earlier run is removed; a
socket that another process still listens on, or any other kind of file, is an
error. The file is created with mode \fB660\fR (see \fB\-m\fR) and removed again
on exit. A UNIX socket path holds at most 103 bytes.
.TP 8
\fB\-r file\fR
Read a Frame Streams file. \fB\-\fR is standard input.
.TP 8
\fB\-z names\fR
Comma-separated zone names (\fBexample.test,example.org\fR). May be given more
than once. Names are written without escapes; \fB.\fR is the root zone.
.TP 8
\fB\-Z file\fR
Zone names, one per line; \fB#\fR starts a comment.
.TP 8
\fB\-i seconds\fR
Summary interval for \fB\-s\fR, 1 to 86400 (default 60). Not used with
\fB\-r\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-f format\fR
\fBtext\fR (default), \fBjson\fR (one line per summary) or \fBprom\fR
(Prometheus text exposition format).
.TP 8
\fB\-o file\fR
Write each summary to this file, replacing the previous one: the text is written
to \fBfile.tmp.<pid>\fR in the same directory and renamed, so a reader (for
instance the node_exporter textfile collector) never sees a half-written
file. The file is created with mode 644. Without \fB\-o\fR, summaries are
appended to standard output.
.TP 8
\fB\-k n\fR
Keys kept per dimension, 1 to 1000000 (default 10000).
.TP 8
\fB\-n n\fR
Entries printed per dimension, most frequent first (default 20; 0 = all that are
kept).
.TP 8
\fB\-t type\fR
\fBresponse\fR (default) or \fBquery\fR: the message type counted for zone,
qname, qtype and client.
.TP 8
\fB\-4 bits\fR, \fB\-6 bits\fR
Client prefix lengths, 0 to 32 (default 24) and 0 to 128 (default 56).
.TP 8
\fB\-m mode\fR
Octal permission of the socket file (default \fB660\fR). The sender
(\fBkaridns\fR) must be able to connect to the socket: the Backend of
\fBkaridns\fR connects through its unprivileged broker, so give the socket a
group or mode that the user of \fBkaridns\fR can write to.
.TP 8
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print the version.
.TP 8
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print the usage.
.PP
On \fBSIGINT\fR or \fBSIGTERM\fR the summary of the interval in progress is
written and the program exits.
.PP
The exit status is 0, 1 for a runtime error (a bad file, a protocol error in a
file, a failing output) and 2 for a usage error.
.SH OUTPUT
.SS text
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-tapstat 2026\-11\-11T12:00:00Z \- 2026\-11\-11T12:01:00Z (60.0 s)
frames 1204  bad 0  queries 0  responses 1204  other\-messages 0  no\-question 0  malformed 0
zone: events 1204  keys 2  evictions 0
         900  example.test.
         304  other
qname: events 1204  keys 87  evictions 0
         211  www.example.test.
         ...
qtype: events 1204  keys 4  evictions 0
         ...
rcode: events 1204  keys 3  evictions 0
         ...
client: events 1204  keys 5  evictions 0
         610  192.0.2.0/24
         ...
.fi
.RE
.PP
A key that may be over-counted is followed by \fB(may be over\-counted by up to
E)\fR; see "Bounded memory". \fBframes\fR is the number of dnstap frames,
\fBbad\fR those that could not be decoded, \fBother\-messages\fR those of a
message type that is neither query nor response, \fBno\-question\fR messages
with an empty question section, \fBmalformed\fR those whose question could not
be read.
.SS json
One JSON object per summary on one line: \fBstart\fR, \fBend\fR, \fBseconds\fR,
the counters above (\fBframes\fR, \fBbad_frames\fR, \fBqueries\fR,
\fBresponses\fR, \fBother_messages\fR, \fBno_question\fR, \fBmalformed\fR) and
\fBdimensions\fR, an object with \fBzone\fR, \fBqname\fR, \fBqtype\fR,
\fBrcode\fR and \fBclient\fR, each
\fB{"events":N,"keys":N,"evictions":N,"top":[{"key":"...","count":N,"error":N},...]}\fR;
\fBcount\fR may be too high by up to \fBerror\fR (Space-Saving).
.SS prom
Gauges for the interval just ended (the counters restart after every summary, so
they are not Prometheus counters): \fBkaridns_tapstat_interval_seconds\fR,
\fBkaridns_tapstat_last_summary_timestamp_seconds\fR,
\fBkaridns_tapstat_messages{kind=...}\fR,
\fBkaridns_tapstat_events{dimension=...,kind="total"|"evictions"}\fR,
\fBkaridns_tapstat_count{dimension=...,key=...}\fR for the most frequent keys
and \fBkaridns_tapstat_count_error{dimension=...,key=...}\fR for the over-count
bound of each key where it is not 0. Label values are escaped as the format
requires (\fB\e\e\fR, \fB\e"\fR, \fB\en\fR). With \fB\-o\fR into the textfile
directory of node_exporter this gives query statistics without opening another
port.
.SH EXAMPLES
Statistics of two zones as a Prometheus textfile, refreshed every minute:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-tapstat \-s /var/run/karidns/tapstat.sock \-z example.test,example.org \-i 60 \-f prom \-o /var/tmp/karidns_tapstat.prom
.fi
.RE
.PP
with, in \fBkaridns.conf\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dnstap {
    socket "/var/run/karidns/tapstat.sock";
    log\-responses yes;
};
.fi
.RE
.PP
The 50 most asked names of a recorded Frame Streams file, as JSON:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
karidns\-tapstat \-r queries.fstrm \-f json \-n 50
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBkaridns(8)\fR, \fBdag\-replay(1)\fR (replays the same dnstap files), dnstap
<https://dnstap.info/>, the Frame Streams protocol (libfstrm,
\fBfstrm_capture\fR).
